fter a router restart, a tombstone that had its units filled in (via submit) would be loaded... but one that hasn't had a submit yet would be skipped.\n\nActually, let me re-read the submit handler for precancelled jobs (lines 65-71):\n\n```python\nif job is not None and job[\"precancelled\"] and job[\"units\"] is None:\n    job[\"units\"] = units\n    job[\"duration\"] = duration\n    self.store.put(job)\n    ...\n    return {\"accepted\": True, \"state\": job[\"state\"]}  # state is CANCELLED\n```\n\nThis correctly keeps the job CANCELLED. So no worker should ever get a lease on it.\n\nBut the violation says the GPU op was *accepted* at t=40. This means a worker submitted it. Let me think about what could cause this...\n\nThe `_apply_report` function at line 264:\n```python\nelif state == \"CANCELLED\" or job[\"cancel\"]:\n    job[\"state\"] = models.CANCELLED\n```\n\nWhen a GPU op finishes CANCELLED and `job[\"cancel\"]` is True, the job becomes CANCELLED. That's fine.\n\nBut what about the `_lease_monitor` at line 309: when a lease expires, it sets `job[\"state\"] = models.QUEUED`. If a job had `cancel=True` but was LEASED, and the lease expires, it goes back to QUEUED with `cancel=True`. Then it gets re-granted. The new holder sees `mode=cancel` and cancels the GPU op. That's the intended flow.\n\nWait \u2014 let me look at the precancel case more carefully. The tombstone has `cancel=True` (line 111 in cancel handler sets it). But actually, looking at `tombstone()` in models.py line 68-73:\n\n```python\ndef tombstone(tenant, job_id, seq):\n    job = new_job(tenant, job_id, None, None, seq)\n    job[\"state\"] = CANCELLED\n    job[\"precancelled\"] = True\n    return job\n```\n\n`new_j"}
